<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dalmatia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dalmatia is a historical region along the eastern Adriatic coast, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. It came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule in the nineteenth century. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> it was one of the predominantly rural and less developed provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dalmatia.jpg" alt="Dalmatia" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DIREKTOR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dalmatia_(Kotor).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dalmatia is a narrow belt of land on the eastern shore of the Adriatic Sea, stretching from the island of Rab in the north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a> in the south. The Dalmatian Hinterland ranges in width from about 50 kilometres in the north to a few kilometres in the south and is mostly covered by the rugged Dinaric Alps. Seventy-nine islands and about 500 islets run parallel to the coast, the largest being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brac">Brač</a>, Pag and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Hvar</a>. The coast has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate, and olives and grapes grow there. The name comes from the Dalmatae, an Illyrian tribe that lived in the area in the 1st millennium BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was part of an Illyrian kingdom between the 4th century BCE and the Illyrian Wars, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> south of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a>. The province of Illyricum was formally established around 32-27 BCE. In 9 CE the Dalmatians rose in the last of a series of revolts, together with the Pannonians; after it was crushed, Illyricum was split in 10 CE into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and Dalmatia. Urban centres were almost completely romanised, while in the countryside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> kept their language, gods and tribal organisation.</p><p>From the middle of the 6th century Slavic migrations pushed the Romance-speaking population to the coast and islands. By 619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> had ruined the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a>, which allowed settlement in the nearby palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and other Slavs founded their own territories. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> was founded in 925. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> held parts of Dalmatia from 1000 to 1358 and from 1420 to 1797, while the Republic of Ragusa existed from 1358 to 1808. Most of the hinterland fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the early 16th century, and Venice later reconquered it.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 Dalmatia was granted to the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as the Kingdom of Dalmatia. In 1848 Croatian demands for union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, supported above all by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, were blocked by the March Constitution. Until 1909 Italian and Croatian were both official languages; by 1910 all but one of the 82 urban communities had a Slav majority. After the First World War Dalmatia became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dalmatia is a historical region only and has no formal status in Croatian law, so its extent rests largely on the borders of the Austrian kingdom. It roughly corresponds to the four southernmost Croatian counties, with seats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>, Šibenik, Split and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. Split is the largest city, followed by Zadar, Šibenik and Dubrovnik. The major rivers are the Zrmanja, Krka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetina">Cetina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dalmatia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The plains of Hungary were among the great grain-producing regions of Europe, and Galicia, Bukovina, <b>Dalmatia</b>, Transylvania and, after 1878, Bosnia-Herzegovina had predominantly rural economies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ante Pavelić, a former Zagreb deputy, went into exile in Italy, where Benito Mussolini gave the Ustaše training grounds in exchange for Pavelić's agreement that <b>Dalmatia</b> would go to Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1225 the territory was divided between a Ban of Croatia and <b>Dalmatia</b> and a Ban of Slavonia; the offices were often held by one person after 1345 and merged by 1476.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Wars with Byzantium over Syrmia and <b>Dalmatia</b> ended in defeat at the Battle of Sirmium in 1167, and repeated campaigns in Galicia brought no lasting gains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Louis I they campaigned widely: in Naples in 1347–1350 to avenge the murder of his brother Andrew, against Venice, which ceded <b>Dalmatia</b> in the Treaty of Zadar in 1358, and in the Balkans, where Bosnia, Serbian lords, Wallachia and Moldavia were drawn into Hungarian suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The country was partitioned: Germany annexed northern Slovenia and occupied Serbia, Italy annexed Ljubljana and much of <b>Dalmatia</b> and controlled Montenegro and, through Albania, most of Kosovo, Hungary took Bačka and Baranja, Bulgaria took most of Macedonia, and the Independent State of Croatia received Bosnia-Herzegovina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
